  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An active weather pattern is likely from late \non Thanksgiving into next weekend. Forecast confidence on any \nparticular weather system is low, but travelers should continue \nto monitor this period for potential travel disruptions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe general weather pattern from Thanksgiving through the \nweekend is favorable for active weather. This will likely impact\nholiday travel. \n\nUpper low over the nrn Plains will be diving south into the MO\nValley today. At the same time a compact s/wv will be moving\nthru the srn Plains and induce cyclogenesis over FL this\nafternoon. This low will eventually get absorbed in the northern\nstream with the parent low pressure sitting up near the OH/PA/NY\nborder by 12z Fri. \n\nStill some question as to whether a triple point will develop\nalong the coast. Guidance has started to back off on this\noccurring but cannot rule out the possibility that it will\ndevelop. There will likely be a coastal front setting up Friday\nmorning or afternoon, depending on the model. There are 2\ndifferent camps on model speeds and pcpn timing. The first is\nthe GFS/RRFS with a faster movement to both the pcpn and the LLJ\nwhile the second camp is the EC/CMC. Given the GFS bringing in\npcpn too fast and not having a feel yet for the new RRFS, have\nopted to side more toward the EC/CMC. To be fair the GFS has\ntrended slower and the EC has trended quicker with the precip.\nAll will depend on the strength of the high pressure parked over\nus today and how quickly it shifts east into the Maritimes. \n\nGuidance is slowly beginning to agree that the bulk of the pcpn\nmoves into Downeast and the Bangor Region around midnight\nThursday night, spreading north into the remainder of the CWA\naround daybreak. Ptypes remain in question with surface temps\nright around freezing to the north of the Central Highlands\nwithin 2-3 hours of the onset of pcpn. Uncertainty lies in how\nmuch the dry air can overcome the start of the pcpn and allow\ntemps to rise above freezing or will it wet bulb down enough to\nproduce snow at onset before warming quickly enough aloft to \nproduce fzra? \n\nAir temps warm into the upr 30s on Thursday and will that be\nenuf to warm road temps or will they drop below freezing again \nFriday morning. Another caveat is will pcpn fall heavy enuf acrs\nthe north to prevent much in the way of accretion on area \nroadways? As the event gets closer the more questions there are\nregarding ptypes and impacts on those traveling \n\nAdditional chances for wintry mix/ snow showers are possible \nthrough the weekend, though confidence is low at this time. In \nsummary, it is still early, but be aware of the potential for \nactive weather that could impact travel Thanksgiving evening \nthrough the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dangerous marine conditions and possible coastal  \nimpacts around the time of the midday high tide on Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nMinor coastal flooding is possible mainly around the midday Friday\nhigh tide. This could come as overwash/wave runup on more \nexposed south/east facing shorelines. One mitigating factor is \nthat it tentatively appears that the greatest storm surge comes \nnear low tide Friday morning in between high tide cycles. We \nwill have to keep an eye on timing, though, as this could \ninfluence the degree of coastal flooding. Continue to check back\nfor updates.",
